(Title) Tj
T*
(Mobile App-Delivered Respiratory Rehabilitation in Chronic Lung Disease: Efficacy and) Tj
T*
(Implementation in Primary Care Settings) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Ninety participants with chronic respiratory conditions were randomised to receive either) Tj
T*
(a 12-week smartphone application-guided rehabilitation curriculum or conventional) Tj
T*
(outpatient pulmonary management. The mobile app cohort completed supervised exercise) Tj
T*
(protocols, breathing training, and educational modules accessible via their phones.) Tj
T*
(Exercise testing conducted at baseline and post-intervention revealed the app-based group) Tj
T*
(experienced greater improvements in capacity and objective breathing assessments compared) Tj
T*
(to usual care. Functional disability scores measuring respiratory symptom burden improved) Tj
T*
(significantly in the intervention arm. A substantial proportion of app users) Tj
T*
(\(approximately 80%\) rated the interface as intuitive, and over 60% reported perceived) Tj
T*
(benefit for breathing comfort. However, adherence was variable, with fewer than half) Tj
T*
(completing all prescribed sessions. Health expenditures between groups remained) Tj
T*
(comparable. Feasibility implementation in four community primary care clinics demonstrated) Tj
T*
(similar benefit profiles. No treatment-related injuries or disease flare-ups were) Tj
T*
(documented. These findings support the effectiveness of app-based pulmonary rehabilitation) Tj
T*
(for improving clinical outcomes, particularly in motivated and adherent participants.) Tj
T*
(Future efforts should address strategies to sustain long-term engagement with digital) Tj
T*
(rehabilitation platforms.) Tj
ET
